  "title": "Dua of the Prostration of Thanksgiving",
  "data": "Recite any of these in the thanksgiving prostration ([Sajdah al-Shukr](I19)).\nRepeat 100 times:\nشُكْرًا شُكْرًا.\nSHUKRAN SHUKRAA\nThanks! Thanks!\nOr repeat 100 times:\nعَفْوًا عَفْوًا.\nA'FWAN A'FWAA\nPardon! Pardon!\nOr repeat 100 times:\nحَمْدًا لِلّٰهِ.\nHAMDAN LILLAAH\nPraise be to Allah!\nIn the prostration, say:\nيَا رَبَّ الْاَرْبَابِ وَيَا مَلِكَ الْمُلُوْكِ وَيَا سَيِّدَ السَّادَاتِ وَيَا جَبَّارَ الْجَبَابِرَةِ وَيَا اِلٰهَ الْاٰلِهَةِ صَلِّ عَلىٰ مُحَمَّدٍ وَّاٰلِ مُحَمَّدٍ.\nYAA RABBAL ARBAABI WA YAA MALIKAL MULOOKI WA YAA SAYYIDAS SAADAATI WA YAA JABBAARAL JABAABIRATI WA YAA ILAAHAL AALIHATI SALLI A'LAA MUHAMMADIN WA AALI MUHAMMAD\nO Lord of lords, O King of kings, O Master of masters, O Compeller of compellers, O God of gods, bless Muhammad and the Household of Muhammad.\nAsk for what you need, then say:\nفَاِنِّىْ عَبْدُكَ نَاصِيَتِىْ فِىْ قَبْضَتِكَ.\nFA-INNEE A'BDUKA NAASIYATEE FEE QABDATIK\nFor I am Your servant; my forelock is in Your grasp.\nThen pray to Allah, for He forgives all sins and no problem is unsolvable for Him.\nImam al-Baqir (a.s.) would say in prostration, in a tender voice:\nسُبْحَانَكَ اللّٰهُمَّ اَنْتَ رَبِّى حَقًّا حَقًّا\nSUBHAANAKALLAAHUMMA ANTA RABBEE HAQQAN HAQQAA\nGlory be to You, O Allah! You are truly, truly my Lord.\nسَجَدْتُ لَكَ يَا رَبِّ تَعَبُّدًا وَّرِقًّا\nSAJADTU LAKA YAA RABBI TA'ABBUDAN WA RIQQAA\nI have prostrated to You, O my Lord, in worship and in servitude.\nاَللّٰهُمَّ اِنَّ عَمَلِىْ ضَعِيْفٌ فَضَاعِفْهُ لِىْ\nALLAAHUMMA INNA A'MALEE DA'EEFUN FADAA'IFHU LEE\nO Allah, my deeds are weak, so multiply them for me.\nاَللّٰهُمَّ قِنِىْ عَذَابَكَ يَوْمَ تَبْعَثُ عِبَادَكَ\nALLAAHUMMA QINEE A'DHAABAKA YAWMA TAB'ATHU I'BAADAK\nO Allah, protect me from Your punishment on the day You raise up Your servants,\nوَتُبْ عَلَىَّ اِنَّكَ اَنْتَ التَّوَّابُ الرَّحِيْمُ.\nWA TUB A'LAYYA INNAKA ANTAT TAWWAABUR RAHEEM\nand turn to me in mercy; surely You are the Oft-Returning, the Most Merciful.\nImam al-Kazim (a.s.) would say in prostration:\nاَعُوْذُ بِكَ مِنْ نَّارٍ حَرُّهَا لَا يُطْفٰى وَاَعُوْذُ بِكَ مِنْ نَّارٍ جَدِيْدُهَا لَا يَبْلىٰ\nA'OODHU BIKA MIN NAARIN HARRUHAA LAA YUTFAA WA A'OODHU BIKA MIN NAARIN JADEEDUHAA LAA YABLAA\nI seek refuge in You from a Fire whose heat is never quenched, and I seek refuge in You from a Fire whose newness never wears out,\nوَاَعُوْذُ بِكَ مِنْ نَّارٍ عَطْشَانُهَا لَا يُرْوٰى وَاَعُوْذُ بِكَ مِنْ نَّارٍ مَسْلُوْبُهَا لَا يُكْسٰى.\nWA A'OODHU BIKA MIN NAARIN A'TSHAANUHAA LAA YURWAA WA A'OODHU BIKA MIN NAARIN MASLOOBUHAA LAA YUKSAA\nand I seek refuge in You from a Fire whose thirsty are never given drink, and I seek refuge in You from a Fire whose stripped are never clothed.\nFor someone who is ill, say in the thanksgiving prostration after every obligatory prayer:\nيَا رَؤُوْفُ يَا رَحِيْمُ، يَا رَبِّ يَا سَيِّدِىْ.\nYAA RA'OOFU YAA RAHEEMU YAA RABBI YAA SAYYIDEE\nO Most Kind, O Most Merciful, O my Lord, O my Master!\nThen ask for what you need.\nImam al-Sadiq and Imam al-Kazim (a.s.) often said in this prostration:\nاَسْاَلُكَ الرَّاحَةَ عِنْدَ الْمَوْتِ وَالْعَفْوَ عِنْدَ الْحِسَابِ.\nAS'ALUKAR RAAHATA I'NDAL MAWTI WAL A'FWA I'NDAL HISAAB\nI ask You for ease at the time of death and pardon at the time of reckoning.\nImam al-Sadiq (a.s.) would say when he prostrated:\nسَجَدَ وَجْهِىَ اللَّئِيْمُ لِوَجْهِ رَبِّىَ الْكَرِيْمِ.\nSAJADA WAJHIYAL LA'EEMU LIWAJHI RABBIYAL KAREEM\nMy lowly face has prostrated to the Noble Face of my Lord.\nIn prostration, repeat 3 times:\nاِنِّىْ ظَلَمْتُ نَفْسِىْ فَاغْفِرْ لِىْ.\nINNEE ZALAMTU NAFSEE FAGHFIR LEE\nI have wronged myself, so forgive me.\nThe Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) would say when he placed his face on the ground:\nاَللّٰهُمَّ مَغْفِرَتُكَ اَوْسَعُ مِنْ ذُنُوْبِىْ وَرَحْمَتُكَ اَرْجىٰ عِنْدِىْ مِنْ عَمَلِىْ\nALLAAHUMMA MAGHFIRATUKA AWSA'U MIN DHUNOOBEE WA RAHMATUKA ARJAA I'NDEE MIN A'MALEE\nO Allah, Your forgiveness is vaster than my sins, and Your mercy is more hoped for by me than my deeds;\nفَاغْفِرْ لِىْ ذُنُوْبِىْ يَا حَيًّا لَا يَمُوْتُ.\nFAGHFIR LEE DHUNOOBEE YAA HAYYAN LAA YAMOOT\nso forgive me my sins, O Ever-Living Who never dies.\nWhen an ordeal or agony befalls you, prostrate on the ground and say:\nيَا مُذِلَّ كُلِّ جَبَّارٍ يَا مُعِزَّ كُلِّ ذَلِيْلٍ\nYAA MUDHILLA KULLI JABBAARIN YAA MU'IZZA KULLI DHALEEL\nO Humbler of every tyrant, O Honorer of every humble one!\nقَدْ وَحَقِّكَ بَلَغَ مَجْهُوْدِىْ\nQAD WA HAQQIKA BALAGHA MAJHOODEE\nBy Your right, my endurance has reached its limit;\nفَصَلِّ عَلىٰ مُحَمَّدٍ وَّاٰلِ مُحَمَّدٍ وَّفَرِّجْ عَنِّىْ.\nFASALLI A'LAA MUHAMMADIN WA AALI MUHAMMADIN WA FARRIJ A'NNEE\nso bless Muhammad and the Household of Muhammad, and relieve me.\nIn a disaster, misfortune or trial, you may bare your knees and arms to the elbows, press them and your chest to the ground, and pray for relief.\nWhile prostrating, repeat 3 times:\nيَا اَللّٰهُ يَا رَبَّاهُ يَا سَيِّدَاهُ.\nYAA ALLAAHU YAA RABBAAHU YAA SAYYIDAAH\nO Allah! O my Lord! O my Master!\nOr, while prostrating, say in one breath:\nيَا رَبَّاهُ يَا سَيِّدَاهُ.\nYAA RABBAAHU YAA SAYYIDAAH\nO my Lord! O my Master!\nRecite this dua of a prophet who lived among the people of 'Ad:\nيَا رَبِّ مَاذَا عَلَيْكَ اَنْ تُرْضِىَ عَنِّىْ كُلَّ مَنْ كَانَ لَهٗ عِنْدِىْ تَبِعَةٌ\nYAA RABBI MAADHAA A'LAYKA AN TURDIYA A'NNEE KULLA MAN KAANA LAHOO I'NDEE TABI'AH\nO my Lord, what would it cost You to make everyone who has a claim against me pleased with me,\nوَاَنْ تَغْفِرَ لِىْ ذُنُوْبِىْ وَاَنْ تُدْخِلَنِى الْجَنَّةَ بِرَحْمَتِكَ\nWA AN TAGHFIRA LEE DHUNOOBEE WA AN TUDKHILANIL JANNATA BIRAHMATIK\nto forgive me my sins, and to admit me into Paradise by Your mercy?\nفَاِنَّمَا عَفْوُكَ عَنِ الظَّالِمِيْنَ وَاَنَا مِنَ الظَّالِمِيْنَ\nFA-INNAMAA A'FWUKA A'NIZ ZAALIMEENA WA ANAA MINAZ ZAALIMEEN\nYour pardon is only for the wrongdoers, and I am one of the wrongdoers;\nفَلْتَسَعْنِىْ رَحْمَتُكَ يَا اَرْحَمَ الرَّاحِمِيْنَ.\nFALTASA'NEE RAHMATUKA YAA ARHAMAR RAAHIMEEN\nso let Your mercy embrace me, O Most Merciful of the merciful.",
  "merits": "Although many utterances are reported to be said in thanksgiving prostrations, these are the easiest of them.\n\nShukran Shukran: Through a valid chain of authority, Imam al-Rida (a.s.) is reported to have instructed that one may repeat this word one hundred times in thanksgiving prostrations. One may also repeat 'Afwan 'Afwan one hundred times.\n\nHamdan Lillah: In the book of 'Uyun Akhbar al-Rida, it is reported on the authority of Raja' ibn Abi'l-Dahhak that Imam al-Rida (a.s.), on his way to Khurasan, used to prostrate himself after the post-Afternoon Prayer utterances and repeat this phrase one hundred times.\n\nYa Rabb al-Arbab: Al-Kulayni has reported through a valid chain of authority that Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said: The closest position of a servant to Almighty Allah is when he prostrates himself before Him and supplicates Him.\n\nSubhanaka Allahumma: Al-Kulayni has reported through an authenticated chain of authority that Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) said: One night, I saw my father prostrating himself in the mosque. I could hear him saying this with a tender tone.\n\nA'udhu bika min Nar: Through a valid chain of authority, al-Kulayni also reports that Imam Musa al-Kazim (a.s.) used to say this supplicatory prayer while prostrating himself.\n\nYa Ra'ufu Ya Rahim: Through a valid chain of authority, al-Kulayni also reports that a man complained to Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) about an ailment that affected one of his bondwomen, and the Imam taught him this.\n\nAs'aluka al-Raha: Through several valid chains of authority, Imam al-Sadiq and Imam al-Kazim (a.s.) are reported to have very frequently said this supplicatory prayer when they did the thanksgiving prostrations.\n\nSajada Wajhi: Through an authentic chain of authority, it is reported that Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) used to say this statement when he prostrated himself.\n\nInni Zalamtu Nafsi: In some considerable reference books, it is reported that Imam Ali Amir al-Mu'minin (a.s.) said: The most favorite words to Almighty Allah is that when a servant (of Him) prostrates himself, he should repeat this statement three times.\n\nAllahumma Maghfiratuka: In the book of al-Ja'fariyyat, it is recorded through an authentic chain of authority that Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) quoted the Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) as saying this when he placed his face on the ground for prostration.\n\nYa Mudhilla Kulli Jabbar: Al-Qutb al-Rawandi has reported Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) to have said: Whenever an ordeal or agony afflicts you, you may prostrate on the ground and say this. In the book of 'Uddat al-Da'i, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) is reported to have said, \"Anyone who is afflicted by a disaster, a misfortune, or a tribulation may bare his knees and arms to the elbows and stick them, as well as his chest, to the ground and supplicate for the settlement of his problem.\"\n\nYa Allahu Ya Rabbah: Ibn Babawayh, through a valid chain of authority, reports Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) as saying: When a servant repeats this supplicatory prayer three times while prostrating, the Blessed and Exalted Lord will answer him, \"Here I am to respond to you, My servant! Ask for what you need.\" In the book of Makarim al-Akhlaq, it is recorded that when a servant prostrates himself and repeats \"Ya Rabbah Ya Sayyidah\" all in one breath, the Blessed and Exalted Lord will answer him, \"Here I am to respond to you. What do you need?\"\n\nDua of a prophet of 'Ad: In the book of Makarim al-Akhlaq too, Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) is reported to have related that the Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) passed by a prostrating man and could hear him saying this supplicatory prayer. The Holy Prophet (s.a.w.a.) said to him, \"Raise your head. Your prayer has been responded. You have just prayed Allah with the supplicatory prayer of a prophet who lived among the people of 'Ad.\""
